<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tang Code</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">653</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Tang Lü Shuyi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tang Code was the comprehensive penal code of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>, issued with an official commentary, the Tang Lü Shuyi, in 653. It defined crimes and punishments systematically and reflected Confucian principles of hierarchy and family relationships. Accounts of the Tang Dynasty treat it as central to Tang governance, and it was a model for later Chinese dynasties and for legal codes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tang Code grew out of the code of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> of 564, which was itself based on the earlier codes of the Cao-Wei and the Western Jin of 268. It was drawn up in 624 at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang">Emperor Gaozu of Tang</a>, with the aim of smoothing the earlier laws and reducing physical punishments such as mutilation, so as to ease social tensions in the newly pacified Tang territories. It was revised in 627 and 637 under the influence of Emperor Taizong and promulgated in 652, under Emperor Gaozong, with 502 articles in 12 sections, before the commentary was added.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The code combined Legalist and Confucian views of law and was supplemented by civil statutes and regulations. Each offence was tied to a fixed penalty, which was then raised or lowered according to the relation between offender and victim. Within the family this was measured by the kind and length of mourning owed to each degree of kinship; outside it, by rank in a hierarchy headed by the emperor, in which officials stood above ordinary men and they above people of servile status. A slave who harmed his master was punished more severely than a commoner who did the same, and a master who harmed his slave less severely.</p><p>The magistrate's task was to fix the offence as the code defined it and set the penalty. If evidence and testimony did not decide a case, he could ask higher officials for permission to use judicial torture: no more than 200 blows with a prescribed interrogation stick, in up to three sessions at least twenty days apart. If the accused confessed nothing, the accuser was tortured in turn. Penalties ranged from beating with a rattan or a bamboo stick through penal labour and exile to death by strangulation or decapitation. Private households found with the Luoshu Square or the Yellow River Map, used in divination, faced two years of forced labour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Tang Code is the earliest Chinese code to survive complete, and it became the basis for later dynastic codes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. The sinologist Jacques Gernet called it &quot;an admirable composition of faultless logic&quot;, and Wallace Johnson and Denis Twitchett described it as &quot;a very rational system of justice&quot;.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tang Code” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Tang Code</b>, first issued in 624 and supplemented in 653 by an authoritative official commentary (Tang Lü Shuyi), was a comprehensive legal compilation that influenced the legal systems of Japan, Korea and Vietnam, and so carried Tang institutions across East Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
